From: WinBoat
Subject: [PATCH] vrend: a new program rebinds the geometry and tessellation stages' buffers too

On a program change vrend marks the constant buffers and sampler views dirty, so the draw binds them again: the
binding points are handed out per program (counting through its stages), and they hold whatever the last program
put there. The loop went from PIPE_SHADER_VERTEX to PIPE_SHADER_FRAGMENT, which in pipe_shader_type are 0 and 1:
the geometry and tessellation stages (2-4) weren't marked, and kept the last program's buffers at their binding
points. 3DMark Vantage's GPU cloth runs a vertex shader with a constant buffer, then geometry shaders with their
own: the geometry shader's first block read the vertex shader's constants (time step and gravity instead of the
primitive count and stiffness), the cloth's constraints went wrong, it blew up within two seconds and the flags
vanished. Every graphics stage is marked now.
---
--- a/src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c
+++ b/src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c
@@ -5880,8 +5880,10 @@
       sub_ctx->prog_ids[PIPE_SHADER_COMPUTE] = 0;
       sub_ctx->prog = prog;
 
-      /* mark all constbufs and sampler views as dirty */
-      for (int stage = PIPE_SHADER_VERTEX; stage <= PIPE_SHADER_FRAGMENT; stage++) {
+      /* mark all constbufs and sampler views as dirty: of every graphics stage, not just up to the fragment shader
+       * (the geometry and tessellation stages come after it in pipe_shader_type), as the new program's binding points
+       * hold what the last program's stages left there */
+      for (int stage = PIPE_SHADER_VERTEX; stage < PIPE_SHADER_COMPUTE; stage++) {
          sub_ctx->const_bufs_dirty[stage] = ~0;
          sub_ctx->views[stage].dirty_mask = ~0;
       }
